  "configuration_id": "config_002",
  "reason": "Evidence: RNA-only (config_011, scvi_n200, singleprofile) achieves 0.520 acc, while image-only (config_013) is very weak at 0.227, and multimodal (config_003) adds only ~1.2% over RNA-only (0.532). This strongly suggests the RNA representation is the dominant signal and the bottleneck for improvement. All three completed experiments use singleprofile with no aggregation, so the effect of multiprofile is entirely unexplored. Question: Does aggregating multiple RNA profiles (avgpool) improve the RNA signal beyond a single profile, holding the encoder (scvi_n200) and modality (RNA-only) constant? Why this is the best next experiment: config_002 changes exactly one design axis (singleprofile→multiprofile + avgpool) relative to config_011, giving a clean ablation. If it improves, it identifies profile diversity as a key lever and justifies investing further in multiprofile multimodal configs. If it does not, it rules out aggregation as a source of gain and redirects budget toward RNA encoder choice (scgpt, pca_n2000) or fusion strategy. This maximizes information per experiment under the limited budget."
}

